(** The documentation of the natives, which have no source in Cesia: cesia doc reads it, and cesia test runs its examples.
    Each one has a block whose first line is its signature. Nothing here is code but the uses. *)
use std/sys
use std/json
use std/regex
use std/random
use std/columns
use std/debug
use std/net
use std/log
use std/metrics
use std/c
use std/lapack
use std/cesia
use std/bug
use std/dict
use std/fiber
use std/list
use std/math
use std/matrix
use std/num
use std/result
use std/text
use std/value
use std/io
use std/path
use std/sqlite3
use std/test

(** io
    Writing out: Print shows values on the standard output. *)

(** bug
    Bugs: Panic and Assert stop the fiber, with a message. A bug is not an error value: an Err is returned, a panic is not. *)

(** text
    Texts: split, join, search, slice and change them, by characters or by bytes. Positions count from 1. *)

(** matrix
    Matrices and arrays of more dimensions, of floats: build them, reshape them, transpose and multiply matrices. *)

(** sys
    Files, folders, processes, the environment and the clock: what a program touches outside itself. Each function says the
    permission it needs: --allow read, write, exec or env, or a path or a name after =. *)

(** json
    JSON text to values and back: objects become dictionaries, arrays lists, and null missing. *)

(** regex
    Regular expressions that stay linear whatever the pattern and the text: no backtracking, so no pattern can hang a
    program. Positions count characters, from 1. *)

(** columns
    The work on whole columns behind std/frame and std/sql, in OCaml: CSV, groups, joins and rows that share their keys. A
    column of integers or floats is stored without a box. *)

(** debug
    The breakpoints and the input of a debugger given to cesia run --debugger: needs the debug permission. *)

(** net
    TCP: listen, accept, connect, read and write, each waiting in its fiber only. Needs --allow net, or net=:PORT to listen on
    this machine only. std/http builds on it. *)

(** c
    C libraries, called from Cesia: open a library, bind its functions by their types, and pass memory, structures and
    callbacks. Calling C is total trust: it ignores every permission and limit, and a wrong signature can crash the program.
    Needs --allow ffi=NAME. *)

(** lapack
    LAPACK, when the machine has it: solve, determinant, QR and eigenvalues of matrices. std/num uses it when it is there,
    and its own code when it isn't. *)

(** cesia
    Cesia about itself: its version, its binary, its libraries, the fingerprints of cesia.lock, cesia.toml read as tables,
    the uses of a source, and the documentation as the docs site reads it. *)

(** sqlite3
    SQLite's natives, which std/sql builds on: pools of connections, statements, cursors and scripts. Loaded at the first
    call, so that a program that doesn't use it never pays for it. Needs read and write on the path of a database, and never
    ffi. *)

(** log
    Structured logs: each event a line of JSON on the standard output, with its time in UTC, its level and its message,
    then the fields given. Under cesia run --workers, a line is written whole, so the lines of two workers never mix. *)

(** metrics
    Counters and histograms of this program, kept in memory, given in the text format that Prometheus scrapes. Under cesia
    run --workers, each worker counts its own. *)

(** math.Pi
    The ratio of a circle to its diameter.

      math.Pi > 3.14 --> true *)

(** list.Sort(xs)
    The elements in increasing order, keeping the order of equal ones.

      list.Sort([3, 1, 2]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** sys.ReadInput(count = missing)
    All that the standard input holds, up to its end, in an Ok, or an Err: cat data.csv | cesia run clean.ce. With a count,
    the next count bytes only, fewer at the end of the input and "" after it: for a program that answers messages as they
    come, as cesia lsp does. The fiber waits while it reads, and the other fibers run. It needs no permission: the input
    is the program's own.

      script = "$(test.Temp())/shout.ce"
      sys.Write(script, "use std/sys\nuse std/io\nuse std/text\nio.Print(text.Upper(sys.ReadInput().Value))\n")
      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "'$(cesia.Binary())' run '$script'"], input = "hello")?.Out --> "HELLO\n" *)

(** sys.Exit(code)
    Ends the program at once, with an exit code from 0 to 255: 0 says that all went well, another number that something
    failed, for the shell or the program that ran it. What was printed is written out first. The chapter
    [Files](https://www-celestial.github.io/cesia-lang/files.html#standard-input-and-the-exit-code) uses it.

      script = "$(test.Temp())/stop.ce"
      sys.Write(script, "use std/sys\nuse std/io\nio.Print(\"bye\")\nsys.Exit(3)\nio.Print(\"never\")\n")
      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "'$(cesia.Binary())' run '$script'"]) --> result.Ok({Code: 3, Out: "bye\n", Err: ""}) *)

(** sys.Now()
    The time in seconds since 1970, as a float.

      sys.Now() > 0.0 --> true *)

(** io.Print(...)
    Writes the texts of its arguments on one line, separated by spaces.

      io.Print("total:", 3) *)

(** io.PrintError(...)
    Writes as io.Print does, on the standard error: for what went wrong, which a pipe or a file that takes the output leaves
    out, as in cesia run tool.ce > out.txt.

      io.PrintError("warning:", "no data") *)

(** text.Str(...)
    The texts of its arguments, joined with nothing between.

      text.Str("a", 1, true) --> "a1true" *)

(** value.Text(x)
    The text of a value, as print shows it.

      value.Text(12) --> "12" *)

(** bug.Panic(message)
    Stops the fiber with a user error: a bug, not a value to handle.

      size(n) = if n >= 0 n else bug.Panic("negative size") end
      size(3) --> 3
      size(-1) --> error *)

(** bug.Assert(condition, message = missing)
    Does nothing if the condition is true. Otherwise it panics, with the message if there is one.

      bug.Assert(1 < 2) --> missing *)

(** sys.Args
    The command-line arguments after the script, as an array of texts.

      name = if value.Len(sys.Args) > 0 sys.Args[1] else "world" end *)

(** cesia.Doc(name)
    The signature and the comment of a name given as text ("sort", "num.Solve", "date.Date.Year") or of a function, or missing if
    there is none. A method of the running program is found by its text too: "Rect.Area", "Rect:Square".

      text.Contains("One more", cesia.Doc("math.Inc")) --> true *)

(** cesia.DocNames(module = missing)
    For tools: the public names documented in a module, or the names of all the modules without an argument.

      "math.Inc" in cesia.DocNames("std/math") --> true *)

(** fiber.Spawn(f)
    Starts f() in a new fiber and gives a task to wait for.

      task = fiber.Spawn(() -> 6 * 7)
      fiber.Wait(task) --> result.Ok(42) *)

(** fiber.Wait(task, timeout = missing)
    The result of a task, once it is done: an Ok of its value, or an Err if the fiber panicked. With timeout, it waits at
    most that many seconds: then an Err of kind "timeout", and the task runs on. A timeout of 0 never waits.
    A task stopped by fiber.Cancel gives an Err of kind "cancelled".

      fiber.Wait(fiber.Spawn(() -> 1)) --> result.Ok(1)
      fiber.Wait(fiber.Spawn(() -> sys.Sleep(1)), timeout = 0.01).Error.Kind --> "timeout" *)

(** fiber.Cancel(task)
    Asks the fiber of a task to stop, and doesn't wait for it. It stops at its next wait (sys.Sleep, Receive, Select, a
    Send on a full channel, the network, the database, Wait) or at the next checkpoint of a long computation, where it
    lets the others run; a fiber that cancels itself stops at once. Wait then gives an Err of kind "cancelled", whose File
    and Line tell where it was. A task that has ended stays as it was. What the fiber had to do after its wait, it doesn't
    do: code that must end, as a transaction, runs in a fiber of its own.

      task = fiber.Spawn(() -> sys.Sleep(10))
      fiber.Cancel(task)
      fiber.Wait(task).Error.Kind --> "cancelled" *)

(** fiber.Channel(size = missing)
    A new channel: a queue of values between fibers. With a size, it holds that many values at most, and Send waits while
    it is full: a fast sender goes at the pace of its receiver. A size of 0 holds none: Send waits for a receiver to take
    its value.

      ch = fiber.Channel()
      fiber.Spawn(() -> fiber.Send("done", ch))
      fiber.Receive(ch) --> result.Ok("done")
      small = fiber.Channel(size = 1)
      fiber.Send(1, small)
      fiber.Spawn(() -> fiber.Send(2, small))
      fiber.Receive(small) --> result.Ok(1)
      fiber.Receive(small) --> result.Ok(2) *)

(** fiber.Send(value, ch)
    Puts a value at the end of a channel, and wakes the first fiber waiting on it. On a channel full to its size, it waits
    for room first; else it never waits.

      ch = fiber.Channel()
      fiber.Send(1, ch)
      fiber.Send(2, ch)
      fiber.Receive(ch) --> result.Ok(1) *)

(** fiber.Receive(ch, timeout = missing)
    An Ok of the oldest value of a channel. With none, the fiber waits for one, at most timeout seconds when given: then an Err of kind "timeout". A timeout of 0 never waits.
    A channel closed with nothing left in it gives an Err of kind "closed" at once.

      ch = fiber.Channel()
      fiber.Send("a", ch)
      fiber.Receive(ch) --> result.Ok("a")
      fiber.Receive(ch, timeout = 0).Error.Kind --> "timeout" *)

(** fiber.Select(chs, timeout = missing)
    Receives from the first channel of a list that has a value: an Ok of {Channel, Value}. The channels are tried in the
    order of the list, so an earlier one goes first. With none, the fiber waits for one, at most timeout seconds when
    given: then an Err of kind "timeout"; a timeout of 0 never waits. A channel closed and empty, met before any value,
    gives an Err of kind "closed" with its Channel. For a single channel, fiber.Receive.

      jobs = fiber.Channel()
      stop = fiber.Channel()
      fiber.Send("job 1", jobs)
      fiber.Select([stop, jobs]).Value.Value --> "job 1"
      fiber.Close(stop)
      fiber.Select([stop, jobs]).Error.Kind --> "closed"
      fiber.Select([jobs], timeout = 0).Error.Kind --> "timeout" *)

(** fiber.Close(ch)
    Says that nothing more will be sent on a channel. The values already in it still come; then Receive gives an Err of
    kind "closed" at once, and a for loop over the channel ends. Sending on it then, or closing it again, is a bug, as is
    a Send that was waiting for room.

      ch = fiber.Channel()
      fiber.Send(1, ch)
      fiber.Send(2, ch)
      fiber.Close(ch)
      [v * 10 for v in ch] --> [10, 20]
      fiber.Receive(ch).Error.Kind --> "closed" *)

(** value.Len(x)
    The number of elements of an array, of characters of a text, of pairs of a dictionary, or of fields of a struct; 1 for any other value.

      value.Len("héllo") --> 5 *)

(** value.Size(x, dim = missing)
    The size of each dimension of an array, as a list: [rows, cols] for a list or a matrix, and one more size for each
    dimension past two. With dim, the size of that dimension only. A list has one row.

      value.Size([1, 2, 3], 2) --> 3
      value.Size(matrix.Zeros(2, 3, 4)) --> [2, 3, 4] *)

(** matrix.Reshape(shape, xs)
    The same elements, filled column by column into a new shape, a list of sizes: [rows, cols], or more for an array of
    more dimensions. The count must stay the same.

      value.Size(matrix.Reshape([2, 2], [1, 2, 3, 4])) --> [2, 2]
      value.Text(matrix.Reshape([2, 2, 2], 1..8)) --> "[1 3; 2 4;;; 5 7; 6 8]" *)

(** matrix.Zeros(size, ...)
    An array of 0.0: a list of n with one size, rows by columns with two, and one more dimension for each size past them.

      matrix.Zeros(2) --> [0.0, 0.0]
      value.Size(matrix.Zeros(2, 3, 4)) --> [2, 3, 4] *)

(** matrix.Ones(size, ...)
    An array of 1.0: a list of n with one size, rows by columns with two, and one more dimension for each size past them.

      matrix.Ones(2) --> [1.0, 1.0] *)

(** list.Fill(value, size, ...)
    An array where every element is the value: a list of n with one size, rows by columns with two, and one more dimension
    for each size past them.

      list.Fill("a", 2) --> ["a", "a"] *)

(** matrix.Matmul(a, b)
    The matrix product of two arrays of two dimensions, as floats. The columns of a must match the rows of b.

      matrix.Matmul(matrix.Ones(1, 2), matrix.Ones(2, 1))[1] --> 2.0 *)

(** matrix.Transpose(m)
    The array of two dimensions with rows and columns swapped.

      value.Size(matrix.Transpose([1, 2, 3])) --> [3, 1] *)

(** list.Sum(xs, dim = missing)
    The sum of the elements of an array. With dim, the sums along that dimension, which the result no longer has: a list
    of the sum of each column of a matrix with dim = 1, of each row with dim = 2.

      list.Sum([1, 2, 3]) --> 6
      list.Sum([1 2; 3 4], dim = 1) --> [4, 6]
      list.Sum([1 2; 3 4], dim = 2) --> [3, 7] *)

(** list.Mean(xs, dim = missing)
    The mean of the elements of an array, which is nan if it is empty. With dim, the means along that dimension, as
    list.Sum gives the sums.

      list.Mean([1, 2, 3]) --> 2
      list.Mean([1 2; 3 4], dim = 1) --> [2.0, 3.0] *)

(** list.Min(xs)
    The smallest element of an array, or with two arguments list.Min(a, b), the smaller of each pair. A missing value gives missing.

      list.Min([3, 1, 2]) --> 1 *)

(** list.Max(xs)
    The largest element of an array, or with two arguments list.Max(a, b), the larger of each pair. A missing value gives missing.

      list.Max([3, 1, 2]) --> 3 *)

(** list.Any(mask)
    Is some element of an array of booleans true?

      list.Any([false, true]) --> true *)

(** list.All(mask)
    Are all the elements of an array of booleans true?

      list.All([true, false]) --> false *)

(** math.Abs(x)
    The absolute value of a number, element by element for an array.

      math.Abs(-3) --> 3 *)

(** math.Sign(x)
    -1, 0 or 1 by the sign of a number, element by element for an array.

      math.Sign(-3) --> -1 *)

(** math.Sqrt(x)
    The square root, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Sqrt(9) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Exp(x)
    e to the power x, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Exp(0) --> 1.0 *)

(** math.Log(x)
    The natural logarithm, as a float, element by element for an array.

      math.Log(1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Sin(x)
    The sine of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Sin(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Cos(x)
    The cosine of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Cos(0) --> 1.0 *)

(** math.Tan(x)
    The tangent of an angle in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Tan(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Asin(x)
    The arc sine, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Asin(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Acos(x)
    The arc cosine, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Acos(1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Atan(x)
    The arc tangent, in radians, element by element for an array.

      math.Atan(0) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Atan2(y, x)
    The angle in radians of the point (x, y), from -pi to pi.

      math.Atan2(0, 1) --> 0.0 *)

(** math.Floor(x)
    The largest whole number not above x. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Floor(2.7) --> 2.0 *)

(** math.Ceil(x)
    The smallest whole number not below x. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Ceil(2.1) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Round(x)
    The nearest whole number, halves away from zero. An integer stays an integer.

      math.Round(2.5) --> 3.0 *)

(** math.Div(a, b)
    The integer quotient of a by b, rounded down. Dividing by 0 is an error.

      math.Div(-7, 2) --> -4 *)

(** math.Mod(a, b)
    The remainder of a by b, with the sign of b, so that a == math.Div(a, b) * b + math.Mod(a, b).

      math.Mod(-7, 2) --> 1 *)

(** math.Nan
    The float that is not a number.

      math.Nan == math.Nan --> false *)

(** math.Near(a, b, rel = 1e-9, abs = 0.0)
    Are two numbers within a tolerance: |a - b| <= max(rel * max(|a|, |b|), abs)? Arrays, maps and structs of the same shape
    are near when each of their elements is; other values when they are equal. Infinities are near only themselves, and
    NaN is near nothing. Against 0, rel can't help: give abs.

      math.Near(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3) --> true
      math.Near(1e-12, 0) --> false
      math.Near(1e-12, 0, abs = 1e-9) --> true
      math.Near([1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0 + 1e-12]) --> true
      math.Near(100, 101, rel = 0.01) --> true *)

(** value.Equal(a, b)
    Are two values equal in content, whatever their kind of array or struct?

      value.Equal([1, 2], [1, 2]) --> true *)

(** value.IsInt(x)
    Is this value an integer?

      value.IsInt(1) --> true *)

(** value.IsFloat(x)
    Is this value a float?

      value.IsFloat(1) --> false *)

(** value.IsNumber(x)
    Is this value an integer or a float?

      value.IsNumber(1.5) --> true *)

(** value.IsBool(x)
    Is this value true or false?

      value.IsBool(true) --> true *)

(** value.IsText(x)
    Is this value a text?

      value.IsText("a") --> true *)

(** value.IsArray(x)
    Is this value an array?

      value.IsArray([1]) --> true *)

(** value.IsDict(x)
    Is this value a dictionary, written {Name: 1} or {"name": 1}, or built by Dict or json.Parse?

      value.IsDict({A: 1}) --> true *)

(** value.IsStruct(x)
    Is this value a struct, declared by a struct? A dictionary is not.

      value.IsStruct(result.Ok(1)) --> true *)

(** dict.Fields(s)
    The fields of a struct as a dictionary, in their order: its public fields, and inside its package its small ones too.
    User.From takes it back.

      dict.Fields(result.Ok(1)) == {Value: 1} --> true *)

(** value.IsFunction(x)
    Is this value a function, native ones included?

      value.IsFunction(math.Inc) --> true *)

(** value.IsMissing(x)
    Is this value missing?

      value.IsMissing(missing) --> true *)

(** dict.Dict(pairs = missing)
    A dictionary from an array of structs with a Key and a Value (see Pairs), or an empty one.

      dict.Dict([{Key: "a", Value: 1}])["a"] --> 1 *)

(** dict.Get(key, d)
    The value of a key in a dictionary, or missing if it is absent.

      dict.Get("a", {"a": 1}) --> 1 *)

(** dict.GetOr(key, default, d)
    The value of a key in a dictionary, or the default if it is absent.

      dict.GetOr("b", 0, {"a": 1}) --> 0 *)

(** dict.Has(key, d)
    Does the dictionary have this key?

      dict.Has("a", {"a": 1}) --> true *)

(** dict.Put(key, value, d)
    A dictionary with the key set to the value. The original is unchanged.

      dict.Put("a", 2, {"a": 1})["a"] --> 2 *)

(** dict.Drop(key, d)
    A dictionary without the key. The original is unchanged.

      dict.Has("a", dict.Drop("a", {"a": 1})) --> false *)

(** dict.Keys(d)
    The keys of a dictionary, as an array, in the order of insertion: writing a key that is there keeps its place.

      dict.Keys({"b": 1, "a": 2}) --> ["b", "a"] *)

(** dict.Values(d)
    The values of a dictionary, as an array, in the order of its keys.

      dict.Values({"a": 1}) --> [1] *)

(** dict.Pairs(d)
    The entries of a dictionary, as an array of structs with a Key and a Value.

      dict.Pairs({"a": 1})[1].Value --> 1 *)

(** list.Append(x, xs)
    A list with x added at the end.

      list.Append(3, [1, 2]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Concat(a, b)
    The elements of a followed by the elements of b.

      list.Concat([1], [2, 3]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Reverse(xs)
    The elements in the opposite order.

      list.Reverse([1, 2, 3]) --> [3, 2, 1] *)

(** list.Take(n, xs)
    The first n elements, or all of them if there are fewer.

      list.Take(2, [1, 2, 3]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** list.Skip(n, xs)
    The elements after the first n.

      list.Skip(1, [1, 2, 3]) --> [2, 3] *)

(** list.IndexOf(x, xs)
    The position, from 1, of the first element equal to x, or missing.

      list.IndexOf(3, [1, 3]) --> 2 *)

(** list.Order(xs)
    The positions, from 1, that put the elements in increasing order. Stable.

      list.Order([30, 10, 20]) --> [2, 3, 1] *)

(** list.Unique(xs)
    The elements without repeats, keeping the first of each.

      list.Unique([1, 2, 1]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** list.Flatten(xs)
    The elements of xs, where each array element is replaced by its own elements, one level deep.

      list.Flatten([[1], [2, 3]]) --> [1, 2, 3] *)

(** list.Zip(a, b)
    The pairs [a[k], b[k]], as many as the shorter list.

      list.Zip([1, 2], [3, 4]) --> [[1, 3], [2, 4]] *)

(** text.Split(sep, s)
    The pieces of a text between the occurrences of a separator, which isn't empty.

      text.Split(",", "a,b") --> ["a", "b"] *)

(** text.Join(sep, xs)
    The texts of the elements, with the separator between.

      text.Join("-", [1, 2]) --> "1-2" *)

(** text.Slice(first, last, s)
    The characters of a text from position first to last, from 1, both included. Outside the text is an error.

      text.Slice(2, 3, "hello") --> "el" *)

(** text.Replace(old, by, s)
    The text with every occurrence of old replaced by another text. Old isn't empty.

      text.Replace("a", "b", "banana") --> "bbnbnb" *)

(** text.Find(part, s)
    The position, from 1, in characters, where part first occurs in a text, or missing.

      text.Find("l", "hello") --> 3 *)

(** text.Contains(part, s)
    Does the text hold the part?

      text.Contains("ell", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.StartsWith(prefix, s)
    Does the text begin with the prefix?

      text.StartsWith("he", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.EndsWith(suffix, s)
    Does the text end with the suffix?

      text.EndsWith("lo", "hello") --> true *)

(** text.Trim(s)
    The text without the spaces and line ends at both ends.

      text.Trim("  a ") --> "a" *)

(** text.Lower(s)
    The text with the letters A to Z in lower case. Other letters are unchanged.

      text.Lower("AbC") --> "abc" *)

(** text.Upper(s)
    The text with the letters a to z in upper case. Other letters are unchanged.

      text.Upper("AbC") --> "ABC" *)

(** text.Repeat(n, s)
    The text n times in a row.

      text.Repeat(3, "ab") --> "ababab" *)

(** text.Lines(s)
    The lines of a text, without their line ends. A last line end opens no empty line.

      text.Lines("a\nb\n") --> ["a", "b"] *)

(** text.PadLeft(n, filler, s)
    The text, filled at the left with the filler up to n characters.

      text.PadLeft(3, "0", "7") --> "007" *)

(** text.PadRight(n, filler, s)
    The text, filled at the right with the filler up to n characters.

      text.PadRight(3, ".", "7") --> "7.." *)

(** text.Chars(s)
    The characters of a text, each as a text.

      text.Chars("héy") --> ["h", "é", "y"] *)

(** text.Char(code)
    The text of one character, from its code point.

      text.Char(65) --> "A" *)

(** text.Code(s)
    The code point of the first character of a text.

      text.Code("A") --> 65 *)

(** text.Number(s)
    The integer or float that a text writes, or missing if it isn't a number.

      text.Number("1.5") --> 1.5 *)

(** text.Decimals(n, x)
    The text of a number with n digits after the point, from 0 to 20, rounded to the nearest, a half away from zero as
    math.Round does; with 0, no point. A float is stored in binary: the one written 2.675 is a little below it, so it gives
    2.67, as in every language that stores floats so.

      text.Decimals(2, 3.14159) --> "3.14"
      text.Decimals(2, 5) --> "5.00"
      text.Decimals(0, 2.5) --> "3"
      text.Decimals(1, -0.25) --> "-0.3"
      text.Decimals(2, 2.675) --> "2.67" *)

(** text.Bytes(s)
    The bytes of a text, as an array of integers from 0 to 255.

      text.Bytes("hi") --> [104, 105] *)

(** text.FromBytes(xs)
    The text made of an array of bytes (integers from 0 to 255).

      text.FromBytes([104, 105]) --> "hi" *)

(** text.Byte(i, s)
    The byte at position i, from 1, of a text.

      text.Byte(1, "A") --> 65 *)

(** text.ByteLength(s)
    The size of a text in bytes.

      text.ByteLength("é") --> 2 *)

(** text.ByteSlice(first, last, s)
    The bytes of a text from position first to last, from 1, both included.

      text.ByteSlice(1, 2, "abc") --> "ab" *)

(** text.ByteFind(part, from, s)
    The byte position, from 1, where part first occurs in a text at or after byte from, or missing.

      text.ByteFind("b", 1, "abc") --> 2 *)

(** math.BitAnd(a, b)
    The bits that are set in both integers.

      math.BitAnd(6, 3) --> 2 *)

(** math.BitOr(a, b)
    The bits that are set in either integer.

      math.BitOr(6, 3) --> 7 *)

(** math.BitXor(a, b)
    The bits that are set in exactly one integer.

      math.BitXor(6, 3) --> 5 *)

(** math.BitNot(a)
    The integer with all its bits flipped.

      math.BitNot(0) --> -1 *)

(** math.BitShift(a, n)
    An integer shifted left by n bits, or right if n is negative.

      math.BitShift(1, 3) --> 8 *)

(** json.Parse(s)
    Reads JSON text: an Ok of the value (objects become dictionaries, null becomes missing), or an Err with the kind "json" and the place of the mistake.

      json.Parse("[1, 2]") --> result.Ok([1, 2]) *)

(** json.Text(x)
    The JSON text of a value, on one line. A function, nan or an infinite float is an error.

      json.Text([1, "a"]) --> "[1,\"a\"]" *)

(** json.Pretty(x)
    The JSON text of a value, with line breaks and two spaces of indent.

      json.Pretty([1]) --> "[\n  1\n]" *)

(** regex.Test(pattern, s)
    Does the pattern match somewhere in the text?

      regex.Test("^a", "abc") --> true *)

(** regex.Find(pattern, s)
    The first match as a struct with Text, Start, Stop (positions in characters, from 1) and Groups, or missing.

      regex.Find("[0-9]+", "ab12c").Text --> "12" *)

(** regex.FindAll(pattern, s)
    Every match, each as in regex.Find, from left to right without overlap.

      value.Len(regex.FindAll("[0-9]", "a1b2")) --> 2 *)

(** regex.Replace(pattern, by, s)
    The text with every match replaced by another text, where $1 to $9 stand for the groups and $$ for a dollar.

      regex.Replace("(a)(b)", "$2$1", "abab") --> "baba" *)

(** regex.Split(pattern, s)
    The pieces of a text between the matches of the pattern.

      regex.Split(",\\s*", "a, b,c") --> ["a", "b", "c"] *)

(** random.Int(low, high)
    A whole number from low to high, both included, each as likely as another.

      random.Int(4, 4) --> 4
      random.Int(1, 6) in 1..6 --> true *)

(** random.Float()
    A float from 0 included to 1 excluded, each as likely as another: random.Float() * 10 is one from 0 to 10.

      random.Float() < 1 --> true *)

(** random.Seed()
    The seed of this run: the one cesia run --seed N gave, or else the one taken from the system at the first draw. The same seed
    gives the same numbers, on every version of Cesia and every system.

      value.IsInt(random.Seed()) --> true *)

(** columns.Kind(xs)
    How an array is stored: "int", "float" or "value".

      columns.Kind([1.5]) --> "float" *)

(** columns.Rows(names, cells, public = false)
    An Ok of a list of maps, one per value.Len(names) cells given row after row; every row of one list of names shares its keys, so a row costs its values only. With public, a SQL name becomes a public Cesia name: created_at gives CreatedAt, id Id; a name that is no identifier, as max(score), stays. Two names that give one key are an Err of kind "names".

      columns.Rows(["id", "name"], [1, "Ana", 2, "Bo"], public = true)?[2].Name --> "Bo" *)

(** columns.Cores()
    The number of processor cores that heavy computations may use.

      columns.Cores() >= 1 --> true *)

(** columns.Groups(cols)
    Groups the rows that share the same values in a list of key columns: a struct with Ids (the group of each row, from 1), Count and Firsts (the first row of each group).

      columns.Groups([[1, 2, 1]]).Ids --> [1, 2, 1] *)

(** columns.Split(groups, xs)
    The values of a column in each group, in order, without the missing ones.

      columns.Split(columns.Groups([[1, 2, 1]]), [10, 20, 30]) --> [[10, 30], [20]] *)

(** columns.Pick(positions, xs)
    The values at these positions, from 1. Position 0 gives missing.

      columns.Pick([2, 0], [5, 6]) --> [6, missing] *)

(** columns.Join(ys, xs, keep)
    The rows of two key columns that match, as a struct with Left (positions in ys) and Right (positions in xs). If keep is true, a row of ys with no match stays, with Right at 0.

      columns.Join([1, 2], [2, 3], false).Left --> [2] *)

(** columns.Positions(mask)
    The positions, from 1, where a mask of booleans is true.

      columns.Positions([true, false, true]) --> [1, 3] *)

(** columns.Present(xs)
    The elements of an array that are not missing.

      columns.Present([1, missing, 2]) --> [1, 2] *)

(** columns.Csv(s, separator)
    The rows of CSV text (RFC 4180, quotes allowed) as lists of texts. The separator is one character.

      columns.Csv("a,b\n1,2\n", ",") --> [["a", "b"], ["1", "2"]] *)

(** columns.ReadCsv(s, separator)
    CSV text with a header row, as a struct with Names and Columns. An empty cell is missing, and a cell that reads as a number is one.

      columns.ReadCsv("a\n1\n", ",").Columns --> [[1]] *)

(** debug.SetBreak(file, line, condition = missing)
    Sets a breakpoint at a line of a file, which stops the program there; with a condition, an expression of the
    variables there as text, only when it is true. One breakpoint per line: a new one replaces it. Needs the debug
    permission.

      debug.SetBreak("app.ce", 20, condition = "total > 100")
      debug.Breaks() --> [{File: "app.ce", Line: 20, Condition: "total > 100"}]
      debug.ClearBreak("app.ce", 20) *)

(** debug.ClearBreak(file, line)
    Removes the breakpoint at a line of a file. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.SetBreak("app.ce", 12)
      debug.ClearBreak("app.ce", 12)
      debug.Breaks() --> [] *)

(** debug.Breaks()
    The breakpoints, each with File, Line and Condition, missing when it has none. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.SetBreak("app.ce", 12)
      debug.Breaks() --> [{File: "app.ce", Line: 12, Condition: missing}]
      debug.ClearBreak("app.ce", 12) *)

(** debug.Watch(code)
    Adds an expression to the watches, which a debugger shows at each stop. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.Watch("total")
      debug.Watches() --> ["total"]
      debug.Unwatch("total") *)

(** debug.Unwatch(code)
    Removes an expression from the watches. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.Watch("total")
      debug.Unwatch("total")
      debug.Watches() --> [] *)

(** debug.Watches()
    The expressions watched, in the order they were added. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.Watch("total")
      debug.Watch("count")
      debug.Watches() --> ["total", "count"]
      debug.Unwatch("total")
      debug.Unwatch("count") *)

(** debug.Eval(code, frame = missing)
    Runs code in the running program and gives the value of its last statement in an Ok, or its error in an Err. With a
    frame of a stop, as stop.Frames[1], the code is an expression of the variables of that frame, of its package and of
    the packages its file uses. Without, it runs at the file level of the main file's package, as at the prompt of cesia
    run --repl: it sees what the code before it defined, and a function it defines again replaces the program's own,
    which goes on with it. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.Eval("price = 5")
      debug.Eval("price * 2") --> result.Ok(10)
      result.IsErr(debug.Eval("price +")) --> true *)

(** debug.Reload()
    Reloads the files of the program changed since they were loaded, as cesia run --watch does: their paths in an Ok, or
    the errors in an Err, and the code of those stays as it was. The code running in a frame goes on as it was; the next
    call runs the new code. Needs the debug permission.

      debug.Reload() --> result.Ok([]) *)

(** debug.IsComplete(code)
    Is the code whole? False when it stops inside a block, a list or an expression, so that a prompt reads another line.

      debug.IsComplete("if x > 1") --> false *)

(** debug.ReadLine(prompt = "")
    A line read from the standard input without its line end, or missing at its end. On a terminal, the prompt shows
    first and the keys edit the line: arrows, Home, End, Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W, and up and down walk the
    lines read before. The other fibers of the program run while it waits, but at a stop of the debugger. Needs the debug
    permission.

      script = "$(test.Temp())/lines.ce"
      sys.Write(script, "use std/debug\nuse std/io\nio.Print(debug.ReadLine())\nio.Print(debug.ReadLine())\n")
      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "'$(cesia.Binary())' run --allow debug '$script'"], input = "Ana\n")?.Out --> "Ana\nmissing\n" *)

(** sys.Read(path)
    The text of a file, in an Ok, or an Err. Needs the read permission.

      file = "$(test.Temp())/read.txt"
      sys.Write(file, "hello")
      sys.Read(file) --> result.Ok("hello")
      result.IsErr(sys.Read("$(test.Temp())/none.txt")) --> true *)

(** sys.Write(path, text)
    Replaces the content of a file with a text, creating it if needed: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission.

      file = "$(test.Temp())/write.txt"
      sys.Write(file, "one") --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Write(file, "two")
      sys.Read(file) --> result.Ok("two") *)

(** sys.Append(path, text)
    Adds a text at the end of a file, creating it if needed: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission.

      file = "$(test.Temp())/log.txt"
      sys.Append(file, "a\n") --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Append(file, "b\n")
      sys.Read(file) --> result.Ok("a\nb\n") *)

(** sys.List(path)
    The sorted names of the entries of a folder, in an Ok, or an Err. Needs the read permission.

      d = "$(test.Temp())/list"
      sys.Mkdir("$(d)/photos")
      sys.Write("$(d)/notes.txt", "")
      sys.List(d) --> result.Ok(["notes.txt", "photos"]) *)

(** sys.Walk(path)
    The paths of every file under a folder, at any depth, in an Ok, or an Err that names the folder that failed: depth first,
    the names of each folder sorted, each path starting with the folder, as photos/2026/a.jpg, but for ".". A folder is
    entered and not listed; a symbolic link is listed, never followed, so no loop can form. Needs the read permission.
    list.Filter(p -> path.Ext(p) == ".jpg", sys.Walk("photos")?) keeps the photos.

      d = "$(test.Temp())/walk"
      sys.Mkdir("$(d)/2026")
      sys.Write("$(d)/2026/a.jpg", "")
      sys.Walk(d) --> result.Ok(["$(d)/2026/a.jpg"]) *)

(** sys.Remove(path)
    Deletes a file or an empty folder: Ok(missing) or an Err. A symbolic link is deleted, never what it points to. Needs the write permission.

      file = "$(test.Temp())/old.txt"
      sys.Write(file, "")
      sys.Remove(file) --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Exists(file) --> false *)

(** sys.Stat(path)
    A dictionary with Size (bytes), Modified (seconds since 1970), IsDir, IsFile and IsLink, in an Ok, or an Err. A symbolic link
    counts as what it points to, and IsLink says that it is one. IsFile is true for a regular file only, not for a folder, a device, a FIFO or a socket: their Size says nothing,
    and reading one can use all the memory or hang. Needs the read permission.

      file = "$(test.Temp())/stat.txt"
      sys.Write(file, "hello")
      sys.Stat(file)?.Size --> 5
      sys.Stat(test.Temp())?.IsDir --> true *)

(** sys.TakeStops(taken)
    Takes the TERM and INT signals from the program, or gives them back, in pairs: for a program that must end by itself
    under --watch, such as a window that saves its state first. With true, TERM and INT no longer end the program, and
    sys.StopAsked tells of them; with false, the program leaves them. Without --watch it does nothing.

      sys.TakeStops(true)
      sys.StopAsked() --> false
      sys.TakeStops(false) *)

(** sys.StopAsked()
    Has a TERM or an INT come since the last call? Only after sys.TakeStops(true).

      sys.StopAsked() --> false *)

(** sys.Stops()
    A channel that the first stop signal, TERM, INT or HUP, closes: each fiber that waits on it, with fiber.Receive,
    hears of the stop at once, and ends its work itself, as a server's requests do. The same channel for the whole
    program. A program that asks for it takes the stop signals on itself: the first closes the channel, the second ends
    the program.

      fiber.Receive(sys.Stops(), timeout = 0).Error.Kind --> "timeout" *)

(** sys.Rename(from, to)
    Moves a file or a folder: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission on both paths.

      d = test.Temp()
      sys.Write("$(d)/draft.txt", "text")
      sys.Rename("$(d)/draft.txt", "$(d)/final.txt") --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Read("$(d)/final.txt") --> result.Ok("text") *)

(** sys.Mkdir(path)
    Creates a folder and the missing folders above it, like mkdir -p: Ok(missing) or an Err. Needs the write permission.

      d = test.Temp()
      sys.Mkdir("$(d)/photos/2026") --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Stat("$(d)/photos/2026")?.IsDir --> true *)

(** sys.Exists(path)
    Does this file or folder exist? Needs the read permission.

      sys.Exists(test.Temp()) --> true
      sys.Exists("$(test.Temp())/none.txt") --> false *)

(** sys.Env(name)
    The value of an environment variable, or missing. Needs the env permission.

      value.IsText(sys.Env("HOME")) --> true *)

(** sys.Sleep(seconds)
    Waits for a number of seconds, letting the other fibers run.

      start = sys.Now()
      sys.Sleep(0.01)
      sys.Now() - start >= 0.01 --> true *)

(** sys.Run(cmd, args = [], input = "", dir = "", timeout = 0)
    Runs a program to its end, giving it the input text, and gives an Ok of a struct with Code, Out and Err, or an Err if it
    can't start. It runs in the folder dir, or in this program's folder if dir is empty. After timeout seconds, if timeout is
    more than 0, it is killed and its Code is -1, as for a program that a signal ended. Needs the exec permission.

      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "echo hi"]) --> result.Ok({Code: 0, Out: "hi\n", Err: ""})
      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "exit 3"])?.Code --> 3
      sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "cat"], input = "data")?.Out --> "data" *)

(** sys.Start(cmd, args = [], dir = "")
    Starts a program that keeps running and gives an Ok of its process. Its output and its errors come as one stream. It runs
    in the folder dir, or in this program's folder if dir is empty. Needs the exec permission.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "echo ready"]).Value
      sys.ReadOutput(proc) --> result.Ok("ready\n")
      d = "$(test.Temp())/start"
      sys.Mkdir(d)
      sys.Write("$(d)/notes.txt", "hi")
      sys.ReadOutput(sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "cat notes.txt"], dir = d)?) --> result.Ok("hi") *)

(** sys.ReadOutput(process)
    The next chunk of what a process wrote, in an Ok. An empty text means that its output is closed.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "echo hi"]).Value
      sys.ReadOutput(proc) --> result.Ok("hi\n")
      sys.ReadOutput(sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "exit 0"])?) --> result.Ok("") *)

(** sys.WriteInput(process, text)
    Sends a text to the input of a process: Ok(missing) or an Err, for instance if the input is closed.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "cat"]).Value
      sys.WriteInput(proc, "hello\n")
      sys.ReadOutput(proc) --> result.Ok("hello\n") *)

(** sys.CloseInput(process)
    Closes the input of a process, so that it sees the end of its input.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "cat"]).Value
      sys.CloseInput(proc)
      sys.WaitExit(proc) --> 0 *)

(** sys.WaitExit(process)
    Waits for a process to end and gives its exit code. The process is finished with after that.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "exit 3"]).Value
      sys.WaitExit(proc) --> 3 *)

(** sys.Stop(process)
    Asks a process to end, with the TERM signal.

      proc = sys.Start("sh", args = ["-c", "read line"]).Value
      sys.Stop(proc)
      sys.WaitExit(proc) --> -1 *)

(** net.Listen(port, host = "127.0.0.1")
    Opens a listening socket: an Ok of the socket, or an Err. Needs the net permission: net=:PORT on this machine only (127.0.0.1,
    ::1, localhost), net=0.0.0.0:PORT or the address itself for an address that other machines reach.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      client = net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server)).Value
      result.IsOk(net.Accept(server)) --> true *)

(** net.Port(socket)
    The port a socket is bound to: the one the system chose for net.Listen(0), which a test uses to take a free port.

      net.Port(net.Listen(0).Value) > 0 --> true *)

(** net.Accept(listener)
    Waits for a connection and gives an Ok of its socket. An Err of the kind "stopped" means that the program was asked to stop.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      client = net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server)).Value
      peer = net.Accept(server).Value
      net.Write(peer, "welcome")
      net.Read(client) --> result.Ok("welcome") *)

(** net.Connect(host, port)
    Opens a connection to a host: an Ok of the socket, or an Err. Needs the net permission.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      result.IsOk(net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server))) --> true *)

(** net.Read(socket)
    Waits for bytes and gives an Ok of the text received. An empty text means that the other side closed.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      client = net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server)).Value
      net.Write(client, "ping")
      net.Read(net.Accept(server)?) --> result.Ok("ping") *)

(** net.Write(socket, text)
    Sends a whole text: Ok(missing) or an Err.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      client = net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server)).Value
      net.Write(client, "ping") --> result.Ok(missing) *)

(** net.Close(socket)
    Closes a socket, which does nothing if it is already closed.

      server = net.Listen(0).Value
      client = net.Connect("127.0.0.1", net.Port(server)).Value
      peer = net.Accept(server).Value
      net.Close(client)
      net.Read(peer) --> result.Ok("") *)

(** log.Info(message, fields = missing)
    Writes a line on the standard output, a JSON object of the time in UTC, the level "info", the message, then the
    fields of a struct or a dictionary: what a server says of itself, read by tools such as jq, Loki or Datadog. Lines are
    buffered as io.Print's are, and keep their order with them.

      log.Info("server ready", {Port: 8080})
      log.Info("shutting down") *)

(** log.Warn(message, fields = missing)
    A line of std/log of the level "warn": something went wrong, and the program goes on.

      log.Warn("slow query", {Ms: 812}) *)

(** log.Error(message, fields = missing)
    A line of std/log of the level "error": something failed.

      log.Error("payment failed", {Order: 7}) *)

(** metrics.Add(name, n = 1, labels = missing)
    Adds n, 0 or more, to a counter, which a struct or a dictionary of labels tells apart from the others of its name:
    each program counts its own, and metrics.Text shows them. A name holds letters, digits and _.

      metrics.Add("orders_total")
      metrics.Add("orders_total", 2)
      metrics.Add("orders_total", labels = {shop: "north"})
      text.Contains("orders_total 3", metrics.Text()) --> true *)

(** metrics.Observe(name, value, labels = missing)
    Counts a value, as the seconds a request took, in a histogram with the buckets of Prometheus, from 0.005 to 10, and
    keeps their sum.

      metrics.Observe("job_seconds", 0.3)
      text.Contains("job_seconds_bucket{le=\"0.5\"} 1", metrics.Text()) --> true *)

(** metrics.Text()
    The counters and histograms of this program in the text format of Prometheus, for a route that it scrapes:
    http.Get("/metrics", r -> http.Plain(metrics.Text())).

      metrics.Add("hits_total")
      text.Contains("# TYPE hits_total counter", metrics.Text()) --> true *)

(** c.Open(name, version = missing)
    Loads a shared library by its short name ("m", "sodium"), trying the usual file names of each system, and on macOS the folders of Homebrew: an Ok of the
    library, or an Err. The names with the version come first, then those without it. Needs the ffi permission.

      m = c.Open("m", version = 6).Value
      cos = c.Fn("cos", ["f64"], "f64", m).Value
      cos(0.0) --> 1.0 *)

(** c.Fn(name, args, ret, lib, callsBack = false)
    Binds a function of a library: an Ok of a Cesia function. The types are given by name ("i32", "f64", "text", "ptr", "void", and so on) or by c.Struct. Set callsBack if the function calls back into Cesia.

      m = c.Open("m", version = 6).Value
      pow = c.Fn("pow", ["f64", "f64"], "f64", m).Value
      pow(2.0, 10.0) --> 1024.0
      result.IsErr(c.Fn("cosine", ["f64"], "f64", m)) --> true *)

(** c.Variadic(name, fixed, rest, ret, lib)
    Binds a function with a variable argument list, like printf: the types of the fixed arguments, then those of the ones used. An Ok of a Cesia function.

      libc = c.Open("c", version = 6).Value
      snprintf = c.Variadic("snprintf", ["ptr", "u64", "text"], ["i32"], "i32", libc).Value
      b = c.Alloc(16)
      snprintf(b, 16, "n=%d", 42) --> 4
      c.Load(0, 4, b) --> "n=42" *)

(** c.Callback(f, args, ret)
    Makes a C function pointer from a Cesia function, to pass where C expects a callback.

      libc = c.Open("c", version = 6).Value
      qsort = c.Fn("qsort", ["ptr", "u64", "u64", "callback"], "void", libc).Value
      b = c.Alloc(3)
      c.Store("cab", 0, b)
      qsort(b, 3, 1, c.Callback((x, y) -> c.Get("u8", 0, x) - c.Get("u8", 0, y), ["ptr", "ptr"], "i32"))
      c.Load(0, 3, b) --> "abc" *)

(** c.Struct(fields)
    A C structure type from the types of its fields, laid out as C does.

      c.Size(c.Struct(["i8", "f64"])) --> 16 *)

(** c.Size(type)
    The size in bytes of a structure type from c.Struct.

      point = c.Struct(["f64", "f64", "i32"])
      c.Size(point) --> 24 *)

(** c.Offset(k, type)
    The offset in bytes of the field k, from 1, of a structure type from c.Struct.

      point = c.Struct(["f64", "f64", "i32"])
      c.Offset(3, point) --> 16 *)

(** c.Alloc(size)
    A block of memory of this many bytes, set to zero, outside the Cesia heap. Free it with c.Free.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Get("i64", 0, b) --> 0
      c.Free(b) *)

(** c.Free(block)
    Releases a block. It can't be used after that.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Free(b)
      c.Get("i64", 0, b) --> error *)

(** c.Get(type, offset, block)
    The number of this type that a block holds at a byte offset, or that a pointer from C points to, as the arguments
    that qsort gives its comparison: C knows the size of that memory, and nothing checks the offset.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Set("i32", 4, -7, b)
      c.Get("i32", 4, b) --> -7 *)

(** c.Set(type, offset, value, block)
    Writes a value of this type into a block at a byte offset.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Set("f64", 0, 2.5, b)
      c.Get("f64", 0, b) --> 2.5 *)

(** c.Store(text, offset, block)
    Copies the bytes of a text into a block at a byte offset.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Store("hi", 0, b)
      c.Get("u8", 1, b) --> 105 *)

(** c.Load(offset, size, block)
    A text of the given number of bytes read from a block at a byte offset.

      b = c.Alloc(8)
      c.Store("hello", 0, b)
      c.Load(1, 3, b) --> "ell" *)

(** c.Text(pointer)
    The text that a pointer from C points to, read up to its zero byte.

      libc = c.Open("c", version = 6).Value
      strerror = c.Fn("strerror", ["i32"], "ptr", libc).Value
      c.Text(strerror(2)) --> "No such file or directory" *)

(** sqlite3.Pool(path, size, wait)
    An Ok of a pool of at most size connections to a SQLite database, created if absent, or to a fresh one in memory with ":memory:", opened when first needed, with WAL for a file and foreign keys. A call that finds them all busy waits at most wait seconds for one. Needs read and write on the path, and no ffi permission. Each connection opens with safe settings: no URI names, defensive, no trusted schema, no extension, nothing attached, texts of 100 MB at most. std/sql is the way to use it.

      db = sqlite3.Pool("$(test.Temp())/app.db", 4, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.Script("create table notes(body)", db) --> result.Ok(missing)
      sys.Exists("$(test.Temp())/app.db") --> true *)

(** sqlite3.Take(pool)
    An Ok of a connection of the pool, waited for if none is free, or an Err with Code "pool" after the wait of the pool, or "closed". sqlite3.Give puts it back.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 0.01).Value
      conn = sqlite3.Take(db).Value
      sqlite3.Take(db).Error.Code --> "pool" *)

(** sqlite3.Give(conn, pool)
    Puts a connection back in its pool, closed if it is broken or if the pool is.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 0.01).Value
      conn = sqlite3.Take(db).Value
      sqlite3.Give(conn, db)
      result.IsOk(sqlite3.Take(db)) --> true *)

(** sqlite3.Query(text, params, db, limit = missing)
    Runs one statement on a connection, or on one of a pool for this call: an Ok of every row, or of the first limit, as maps whose keys are the column names made public names. A busy database, outside a transaction, is waited for in the fiber. A value SQL doesn't take is an Err with Code "convert".

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.Script("create table people(name, age); insert into people values ('Ana', 31), ('Bo', 25)", db)
      sqlite3.Query("select name from people where age > ?", [30], db) --> result.Ok([{Name: "Ana"}])
      sqlite3.Query("select age from people order by age", [], db, limit = 1) --> result.Ok([{Age: 25}]) *)

(** sqlite3.One(text, params, db)
    The first row of a statement, as sqlite3.Query reads it: an Ok of a map, or of missing.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.One("select 6 * 7 as answer", [], db) --> result.Ok({Answer: 42})
      sqlite3.One("select 1 where 0", [], db) --> result.Ok(missing) *)

(** sqlite3.Exec(text, params, db)
    Runs one statement to its end, as sqlite3.Query does: an Ok of {Changed, LastId}.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.Script("create table notes(body)", db)
      sqlite3.Exec("insert into notes values (?)", ["buy bread"], db) --> result.Ok({Changed: 1, LastId: 1}) *)

(** sqlite3.Run(text, params, conn)
    Prepares one statement, from a cache of 128 by text, and binds a value to each ?: missing, a Bool, an Int, a Float or a Text. An Ok of a cursor, or an Err; text after the statement that holds another is an Err.

      conn = sqlite3.Take(sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value).Value
      cursor = sqlite3.Run("select ? + 1 as n", [41], conn).Value
      sqlite3.Next(10, cursor)?.Cells --> [42] *)

(** sqlite3.Next(n, cursor)
    Runs the statement on to n rows: an Ok of {Names, Cells, Done, Changed, LastId}, the cells row after row. NULL is missing, a BLOB a Text of its bytes, a column declared BOOLEAN a Bool. A busy database before the first row, outside a transaction, is waited for in the fiber.

      conn = sqlite3.Take(sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value).Value
      cursor = sqlite3.Run("select 1 as a, 2 as b union all select 3, 4", [], conn).Value
      sqlite3.Next(1, cursor)?.Cells --> [1, 2]
      sqlite3.Next(10, cursor)?.Cells --> [3, 4] *)

(** sqlite3.Script(text, conn)
    Runs several statements, without parameters, on a connection or on one of a pool for this call: an Ok of missing, or the first Err.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.Script("create table t(x); insert into t values (1), (2)", db) --> result.Ok(missing)
      sqlite3.Query("select x from t", [], db) --> result.Ok([{X: 1}, {X: 2}]) *)

(** sqlite3.Close(x)
    Closes a cursor before its end, a connection with its statements, or a pool: its free connections now, the others when they come back. Twice is nothing.

      db = sqlite3.Pool(":memory:", 1, 1.0).Value
      sqlite3.Close(db)
      sqlite3.Take(db).Error.Code --> "closed" *)

(** sqlite3.Version()
    An Ok of the version of the SQLite library, or an Err if there is none.

      text.StartsWith("3.", sqlite3.Version()?) --> true *)

(** lapack.Name()
    The name of the LAPACK library found on this machine, or missing if there is none. The functions below then give
    missing as well, and num falls back on its own code. It has no example, as its answer depends on the machine. *)

(** lapack.Solve(a, b)
    The x of a * x = b for a square a and a b of one column or more, or false if a is singular. Missing without a library,
    and for an order below 32, where the code of num is as fast and gives the same bits on every machine.

      a = num.Eye(32) + matrix.Ones(32, 32)
      x = lapack.Solve(a, matrix.Ones(32, 1))
      value.IsMissing(x) or math.Abs(x[1] - 1 / 33) < 1e-12 --> true
      lapack.Solve(matrix.Ones(32, 32), matrix.Ones(32, 1)) in [false, missing] --> true
      lapack.Solve(num.Eye(2), matrix.Ones(2, 1)) --> missing *)

(** lapack.Det(a)
    The determinant of a square matrix. Missing without a library, and for an order below 32.

      d = lapack.Det(num.Eye(32) + matrix.Ones(32, 32))
      value.IsMissing(d) or math.Abs(d - 33.0) < 1e-9 --> true *)

(** lapack.Qr(a)
    The QR factorization of a matrix with at least as many rows as columns, as a struct with Q and R. Missing without a
    library, and below 32 columns.

      a = num.Eye(32) + matrix.Ones(32, 32)
      f = lapack.Qr(a)
      value.IsMissing(f) or list.Max(math.Abs(matrix.Matmul(f.Q, f.R) - a)) < 1e-9 --> true *)

(** lapack.Eigen(a)
    The eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix, as a struct with Values, from the smallest, and Vectors.
    Missing without a library, and for an order below 32.

      e = lapack.Eigen(num.Eye(32) + matrix.Ones(32, 32))
      value.IsMissing(e) or math.Round(e.Values[32]) == 33.0 --> true *)

(** cesia.Version()
    The version of this Cesia, without what follows a + or a -.

      cesia.IsVersion(cesia.Version()) --> true *)

(** cesia.IsDev()
    For tools: was this Cesia built from its sources, and not by a release? Then its version text ends in +dev.

      shown = sys.Run("sh", args = ["-c", "'$(cesia.Binary())' version"]).Value.Out
      cesia.IsDev() == text.EndsWith("+dev\n", shown) --> true *)

(** cesia.Binary()
    The path of the running binary of Cesia, from the top of the file system.

      path.IsAbsolute(cesia.Binary()) --> true *)

(** cesia.Fingerprint(text)
    For tools: blake2: and the 64 hexadecimal digits of the BLAKE2b-256 of a text, as cesia.lock keeps them.

      cesia.Fingerprint("abc") --> "blake2:bddd813c634239723171ef3fee98579b94964e3bb1cb3e427262c8c068d52319" *)

(** cesia.Compare(a, b)
    For tools: compares two versions number by number, as tags: negative if a comes first, 0 if they are the same,
    positive after. A leading v and what follows a + or a - are left out, and a missing number is 0.

      cesia.Compare("v1.10.0", "1.9") > 0 --> true *)

(** cesia.IsVersion(text)
    For tools: is it numbers between dots, after an optional v?

      cesia.IsVersion("v0.3") --> true *)

(** cesia.Libraries()
    The libraries of this Cesia, as an array of structs with Name and Source: what a project's std/ holds.

      "path" in [l.Name for l in cesia.Libraries()] --> true *)

(** cesia.Toml(text)
    For tools: the tables of a cesia.toml or cesia.lock, in an Ok: each a struct with Path (an array of texts), Line and
    Entries, an entry a struct with Key, Value and Line. An Err of {Line, Message} for a text that isn't the TOML Cesia
    reads.

      cesia.Toml("[a]")?[1].Path --> ["a"] *)

(** cesia.Uses(source)
    For tools: the paths of the use lines of a source, as the loader reads them, in an Ok, or an Err of {Line, Message}.
    Nothing runs.

      cesia.Uses("use std/csv")? --> ["std/csv"] *)

(** cesia.SameTree(a, b)
    For tools: do two sources read as the same program, whatever their spaces, line ends and comments? An Ok of a boolean,
    or an Err of {Line, Message} for a source that doesn't read. Nothing runs: cesia fmt asks it before it writes a file.

      cesia.SameTree("x = 1 + 2", "x  =  1 + 2   (* three *)")? --> true
      cesia.SameTree("x = 1 + 2", "x = 1 + 3")? --> false
      cesia.SameTree("x = (", "x = 1").Error.Line --> 1 *)

(** cesia.Check(paths, given = {})
    For tools: what cesia check finds in files, without running any of them: a list of structs with File, Line, Column,
    End (the column after the span), Severity ("error" or "warning"), Message, Hint (or missing) and Source (the line).
    A file of a source folder of its project is read with its package, a test with it, and any other file alone; given
    maps a path to its text when the disk doesn't hold it. Needs the read permission.

      cesia.Check([], {}) --> [] *)

(** cesia.DocEntry(name)
    For tools: what the site of the docs shows of a name or a keyword, or missing: a struct with Name, Module (as
    DocNames lists it, empty for a keyword), Signature, Text (without its examples), Parts (each {Name, Text}: a field,
    a parameter or the result), Examples (the lines that cesia test runs), Builtin (a keyword, or a function written in
    OCaml), File and Line (where it is defined).

      cesia.DocEntry("list.Sort").Builtin --> true
      cesia.DocEntry("match").Module --> ""
      value.IsMissing(cesia.DocEntry("NoSuchName")) --> true *)

(** cesia.Definitions(source)
    For tools: what a source defines at its top, as the parser reads it, in an Ok: a list of structs with Name, Owner (the
    struct of a method, or missing), Kind ("function", "method", "static", "struct", "interface", "value" or "use"), Line
    and Column (where the name is, from 1, in characters), and the Signature and Text of its documentation, "" when it has
    none; a use has the name the code gives the package, and its path as its Signature. An Err of {Line, Message} for a
    source that doesn't read. Nothing runs: cesia lsp finds a name with it.

      cesia.Definitions("x = 1")?[1].Kind --> "value"
      cesia.Definitions("defn (r Rect) Area()\n  (** Its area. *)\n  1\nend")?[1].Text --> "Its area."
      cesia.Definitions("defn (r Rect) Area()\n  1\nend")?[1].Column --> 15
      cesia.Definitions("use std/set as sets")?[1].Signature --> "std/set"
      cesia.Definitions("x = (").Error.Line --> 1 *)

(** cesia.Keywords()
    The reserved words of the language, then it, as, _, ? and .., which mean something in some places only: what a highlighter
    colors, and the words the docs explain.

      "defn" in cesia.Keywords() --> true *)

(** cesia.Asset(name)
    For tools: a file of the look of the docs site, held in the binary: "site.css" or "site.js". Missing for another
    name.

      value.IsText(cesia.Asset("site.css")) --> true
      cesia.Asset("logo.png") --> missing *)
